WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.560
Five steps from any React Native
build to a performance report.

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:10.080
Start with the build you already have,
Android or iOS, release or debug.

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:14.720
It's analyzed right on your machine
and React Native is detected.

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:20.540
Next, one click builds an instrumented
copy of the app with a performance agent

00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:26.500
inside. Pick a simulator or an emulator
and the instrumented build installs

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:27.600
straight from the dashboard.

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:33.560
Choose a cold or hot start,
hit record, and the launch is measured.

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:37.320
Now just use the app the
way your users would.

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:43.200
Every screen you open, every API call,
and every frame is traced as you go.

00:00:45.490 --> 00:00:47.310
Stop and the report is ready.

00:00:48.070 --> 00:00:52.290
Everything rolls up into one score,
so you know where to look first.

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:58.010
Startup is broken into phases,
so you can see where the launch time

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:03.870
goes. Each screen gets its own time to
interactive, its calls and its dropped

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:09.750
frames. Slow, failing and duplicate
requests stand out, with the time they

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:14.270
waste. Excessive re-renders are traced
back to the components that cause them.

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:20.680
Memory is tracked across every
navigation, so growth is easy to spot.

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:26.560
The timeline lines it all up, so
cause and effect sits side-by-side.

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:31.020
And the biggest bottlenecks are
ranked, each with a suggested fix.

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:38.640
Find what slows your app down and
fix it before your users feel it.
